 ### Variable Gaussian Blurring
-Variable Gaussian Blurring is a reconstruction technique used to reduce visible artifacts from the discrete sampled nature of volume models, specifically false low frequencies caused by the Nyquist limit [S5, unofficial]. It involves applying variable Gaussian blurring to the source volume models before or during reconstruction to high-resolution 2D images [S5, unofficial]. The Nyquist Limit is defined as half the sampling rate, and infinite sampling of continuous surfaces is the only way to completely avoid artifacts [S5, unofficial].
+Variable Gaussian Blurring is a reconstruction technique used to reduce visible artifacts from the discrete sampled nature of volume models, specifically false low frequencies caused by the Nyquist limit [S6, unofficial]. It involves applying variable Gaussian blurring to the source volume models before or during reconstruction to high-resolution 2D images [S6, unofficial]. The Nyquist Limit is defined as half the sampling rate, and infinite sampling of continuous surfaces is the only way to completely avoid artifacts [S6, unofficial].
 
 ### Barycentric Color Interpolation for Voxels
-Barycentric Color Interpolation for Voxels interpolates vertex colors to a voxel center by treating the voxel center as a point inside the triangle and weighting the vertex colors by the areas of the sub-triangles formed with the voxel center [S6, unofficial]. The formula is $c = c_1 \frac{A_2}{A} + c_2 \frac{A_3}{A} + c_3 \frac{A_1}{A}$, where $A_1 = \text{area}(v_1, v_2, P)$, $A_2 = \text{area}(v_2, v_3, P)$, $A_3 = \text{area}(v_3, v_1, P)$, $A = A_1 + A_2 + A_3$, and $P$ is the voxel center [S6, unofficial].
+Barycentric Color Interpolation for Voxels interpolates vertex colors to a voxel center by treating the voxel center as a point inside the triangle and weighting the vertex colors by the areas of the sub-triangles formed with the voxel center [S7, unofficial]. The formula is $c = c_1 \cdot (A_2 / A) + c_2 \cdot (A_3 / A) + c_3 \cdot (A_1 / A)$, where $A_1 = \text{area}(v_1, v_2, P)$, $A_2 = \text{area}(v_2, v_3, P)$, $A_3 = \text{area}(v_3, v_1, P)$, $A = A_1 + A_2 + A_3$, and $P$ is the voxel center [S7, unofficial].
 
 ### Adaptive Interpolation for Isosurface Detection
-Adaptive Interpolation for Isosurface Detection interpolates input volume along rays at a predetermined sampling rate. It uses non-binary classifiers to adjust the acceptable density range for an isovalue surface based on data change rate [S2, unofficial]. When data values change slowly, a narrow range of acceptable values is used; when data values change rapidly, a wide range is used to avoid missing the surface [S2, unofficial]. It supports user selection of interpolation: nearest-neighbor (preview), linear (day-to-day), cubic (publication-quality) [S2, unofficial].
+Adaptive Interpolation for Isosurface Detection interpolates input volume along rays at a predetermined sampling rate. It uses non-binary classifiers to adjust the acceptable density range for an isovalue surface based on data change rate [S3, unofficial]. When data values change slowly, a narrow range of acceptable values is used for the isovalue surface. When data values change rapidly, a wide range is used to avoid missing the surface [S3, unofficial]. It supports user selection of interpolation: nearest-neighbor (preview), linear (day-to-day), cubic (publication-quality) [S3, unofficial].
 
 ### Interpolation Schemes for Projection-Slice Resampling
-Interpolation Schemes for Projection-Slice Resampling are resampling filters applied to extracted projection-slices to control ghosting artifacts and rendering quality during Fourier volume rendering [S7]. Point-sampling (nearest-neighbour) clamps missing samples to the nearest available value, causing ghosting artifacts when the volume is rotated [S7]. Trilinear interpolation reduces replica intensities and ghosting artifacts compared to point-sampling, with similar performance for slice sizes $\le 512^2$ but higher cost for larger volumes [S7]. A Hamming windowed-sinc interpolation filter with width 5 is a high-order reconstruction filter that maximally reduces ghosting artifacts when combined with zero-padding [S7].
+Interpolation Schemes for Projection-Slice Resampling are resampling filters applied to extracted projection-slices to control ghosting artifacts and rendering quality during Fourier volume rendering [S8]. Point-sampling (nearest-neighbour) filter clamps missing samples to the nearest available value, causing apparent ghosting artifacts when the volume is rotated [S8]. Trilinear interpolation filter reduces replica intensities and ghosting artifacts compared to point-sampling, with similar performance for slice sizes $\le 512^2$ but higher cost for larger volumes [S8]. Hamming windowed-sinc interpolation filter with width 5 is a high-order reconstruction filter that maximally reduces ghosting artifacts when combined with zero-padding [S8]. Point-sampling and trilinear interpolation have nearly identical performance for slice sizes $\le 512^2$, but trilinear incurs higher performance overhead for larger volumes [S8].
 
 ## Background
-The study of interpolation in volume rendering originated with the need to render discrete voxel data as continuous images. Early work by Westover (1990) introduced footprint evaluation, highlighting the artifacts of nearest-neighbor interpolation and the benefits of higher-order filters [S3, unofficial]. Levoy (1988) established ray-casting methods, noting that interpolation allows for better isosurface detection than binary classification [S2, unofficial]. The introduction of frequency domain volume rendering by Westover (1993) and Viola (2004) brought signal processing techniques, such as the sinc filter and Fourier transforms, to volume rendering to achieve higher quality at large zoom factors [S1, S4, unofficial]. GPU acceleration techniques, such as fast tri-cubic interpolation, were developed to mitigate the high computational cost of cubic filters by exploiting hardware texture filtering [S1, S3, unofficial].
+The concept of interpolation in volume rendering emerged alongside the development of direct volume ray casting. Early methods relied on nearest-neighbor interpolation due to its simplicity, but this resulted in severe artifacts such as staircase effects and blockiness [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. As hardware capabilities increased, higher-order methods like trilinear and cubic interpolation became feasible, offering improved visual quality by eliminating these artifacts [S4, S5, unofficial]. The introduction of frequency domain techniques, such as those by Westover (1990) and Levoy (1990), allowed for more sophisticated reconstruction filters, including windowed sinc filters, to address aliasing and blurring issues more effectively [S4, S5, unofficial]. Modern implementations often leverage GPU hardware acceleration to optimize these computations, particularly for higher-order filters like tri-cubic B-splines [S1, S5, unofficial].
 
